# *Caparo Industries plc v Dickman* [1990] UKHL 2, [1990] 2 AC 605

Source: *Caparo Industries plc v Dickman* [1990] UKHL 2, [1990] 2 AC 605
(House of Lords). Pinned URL: https://www.bailii.org/uk/cases/UKHL/1990/2.html
In force from 8 February 1990; current. Doctrine — drift = Supreme Court
overrule. Note that *Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire Police*
[2018] UKSC 4 narrowed the application of stage 2; check on update.

ATTRIBUTION (per RULES-ATTRIBUTION.md): Contains public-sector
information licensed under the Open Government Licence v3.0 —
http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/.

## The three-stage formulation — Lord Bridge of Harwich

The modern English test for the existence of a duty of care in negligence
was stated by Lord Bridge in *Caparo*. Quoting the canonical passage at
[1990] 2 AC 605, 617–618:

"What emerges is that, in addition to the foreseeability of damage,
necessary ingredients in any situation giving rise to a duty of care are
that there should exist between the party owing the duty and the party
to whom it is owed a relationship characterised by the law as one of
'proximity' or 'neighbourhood' and that the situation should be one in
which the court considers it fair, just and reasonable that the law
should impose a duty of a given scope upon the one party for the
benefit of the other."

The doctrine therefore requires three elements, conjunctively:

(a) **Foreseeability** — that harm to the claimant was reasonably
    foreseeable;
(b) **Proximity** — that there existed a relationship of sufficient
    proximity (or "neighbourhood") between the parties; and
(c) **Fair, just and reasonable** — that, in all the circumstances, it
    is fair, just and reasonable to impose a duty.

The third limb is the policy gate: even where foreseeability and
proximity are made out, the court may decline to impose a duty where
doing so would be incoherent with the structure of existing duties or
would expose defendants to "liability in an indeterminate amount for an
indeterminate time to an indeterminate class" (Cardozo CJ, *Ultramares
Corp v Touche* 174 NE 441 (1931), as cited in *Caparo*).

*Caparo* itself was decided against the auditor on the third limb: the
House of Lords held that imposing a duty owed by auditors to the
investing public would expose auditors to indeterminate liability and was
not fair, just and reasonable.

## Encoding

The rule of decision is a conjunction of three boolean inputs (the three
limbs as found by the court) producing a single boolean output (duty
owed) plus a count of stages satisfied (used by the disposition layer to
explain the decision).

## *Robinson v Chief Constable of West Yorkshire* [2018] UKSC 4 narrowing

In *Robinson*, the Supreme Court restricted *Caparo*'s three-stage test
to **novel duty-of-care situations**. Where the case falls within an
established category of duty (e.g. a road user's duty to other road
users, an employer's duty to employees, a doctor's duty to a patient),
the court does not run the three-stage test at all; the duty simply
exists by precedent. The three-stage test — and in particular the
"fair, just and reasonable" policy gate — applies only where the duty
asserted is in a category the courts have not previously recognised.

The encoding therefore takes an `is_established_category` input. When
true, the duty is owed by precedent and the three-stage test is not
exercised. When false, the case is novel and all three Caparo limbs
must be made out.

## Encoding

```catala
declaration enumeration CaparoPath:
  -- EstablishedCategory_DutyByPrecedent
  -- NovelCategory_AllStagesMade
  -- NovelCategory_FailsAtStage

declaration enumeration CaparoStage:
  -- Stage_None
  -- Stage_1_Foreseeability
  -- Stage_2_Proximity
  -- Stage_3_FairJustReasonable

declaration structure CaparoFacts:
  ## Robinson v CC West Yorkshire [2018] UKSC 4 gate. If true, the
  ## three-stage test is not run; duty exists by precedent.
  data is_established_category content boolean
  data harm_reasonably_foreseeable content boolean
  data sufficient_proximity content boolean
  data fair_just_reasonable_to_impose content boolean

declaration structure DutyOfCareDisposition:
  data path content CaparoPath
  data n_stages_satisfied content integer
  data first_failing_stage content CaparoStage
  data duty_of_care_owed content boolean

declaration scope CaparoTest:
  input facts content CaparoFacts
  output disposition content DutyOfCareDisposition

scope CaparoTest:
  definition disposition equals
    let foreseeable equals facts.harm_reasonably_foreseeable in
    let proximity equals facts.sufficient_proximity in
    let fjr equals facts.fair_just_reasonable_to_impose in
    let raw_satisfied equals
      (if foreseeable then 1 else 0)
      + (if proximity then 1 else 0)
      + (if fjr then 1 else 0)
    in
    let novel_pass equals foreseeable and proximity and fjr in
    let owed equals
      if facts.is_established_category then true
      else novel_pass
    in
    let chosen_path equals
      if facts.is_established_category then
        EstablishedCategory_DutyByPrecedent
      else (if novel_pass then NovelCategory_AllStagesMade
            else NovelCategory_FailsAtStage)
    in
    ## first_failing_stage names the earliest of the three Caparo
    ## stages that the court found NOT made out, on the novel-duty
    ## path. Stage_None when the duty is owed (established category
    ## OR novel-with-all-three).
    let first_fail equals
      if facts.is_established_category then Stage_None
      else (if not foreseeable then Stage_1_Foreseeability
            else (if not proximity then Stage_2_Proximity
                  else (if not fjr then Stage_3_FairJustReasonable
                        else Stage_None)))
    in
    ## n_stages_satisfied is meaningful only on the novel-duty path.
    ## On the established-category path the three-stage test is not
    ## run; we emit -1 as a sentinel so downstream consumers cannot
    ## misread an established-category disposition as having "passed
    ## N of 3 stages."
    let reported_satisfied equals
      if facts.is_established_category then -1 else raw_satisfied
    in
    DutyOfCareDisposition {
      -- path: chosen_path
      -- n_stages_satisfied: reported_satisfied
      -- first_failing_stage: first_fail
      -- duty_of_care_owed: owed
    }
```

## Application in ADGM

ADGM CFI applies *Caparo* directly via the *Application of English Law
Regulations 2015* in every negligent-misstatement and
professional-negligence claim. *Hedley Byrne v Heller* [1964] AC 465 (a
separate module) supplies the special-relationship rule for liability
for words rather than acts; *Caparo* supplies the duty-of-care framework
into which *Hedley Byrne* fits.

Used implicitly across:
  - ADGM negligent-misstatement / professional-negligence cases in the
    first-pass set (auditor liability, financial advice, etc.).

## Tests

The two test scopes below cover (i) all three stages satisfied and
(ii) the policy-gate fallthrough on which *Caparo* itself was decided.

```catala
#[test] declaration scope TestCaparoEstablishedCategory:
  composite scope CaparoTest
  output disposition content DutyOfCareDisposition

scope TestCaparoEstablishedCategory:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## Robinson narrowing: where the duty is in an established category
  ## (e.g. road-user-to-road-user), the three-stage test is not run.
  definition composite.facts equals
    CaparoFacts {
      -- is_established_category: true
      -- harm_reasonably_foreseeable: true
      -- sufficient_proximity: true
      -- fair_just_reasonable_to_impose: false
    }
  assertion disposition.duty_of_care_owed = true
  assertion disposition.path = EstablishedCategory_DutyByPrecedent
  ## On the established-category path the three-stage count is not
  ## meaningful; the encoding emits -1 as a sentinel.
  assertion disposition.n_stages_satisfied = -1

#[test] declaration scope TestCaparoNovelAllStagesMet:
  composite scope CaparoTest
  output disposition content DutyOfCareDisposition

scope TestCaparoNovelAllStagesMet:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  definition composite.facts equals
    CaparoFacts {
      -- is_established_category: false
      -- harm_reasonably_foreseeable: true
      -- sufficient_proximity: true
      -- fair_just_reasonable_to_impose: true
    }
  assertion disposition.duty_of_care_owed = true
  assertion disposition.n_stages_satisfied = 3
  assertion disposition.path = NovelCategory_AllStagesMade

#[test] declaration scope TestCaparoFailsOnPolicyGate:
  composite scope CaparoTest
  output disposition content DutyOfCareDisposition

scope TestCaparoFailsOnPolicyGate:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## Novel duty; foreseeability + proximity made out, but the policy
  ## gate (fair, just, reasonable) cuts the duty — Caparo itself was
  ## decided this way against the auditor (a novel duty in 1990).
  definition composite.facts equals
    CaparoFacts {
      -- is_established_category: false
      -- harm_reasonably_foreseeable: true
      -- sufficient_proximity: true
      -- fair_just_reasonable_to_impose: false
    }
  assertion disposition.duty_of_care_owed = false
  assertion disposition.n_stages_satisfied = 2
  assertion disposition.path = NovelCategory_FailsAtStage
```
